3	// (2026-07-07-fuse-shared-scope-exit-rotation-live-wiring.md, folded into
4	// Phase 70.1 SC#8).
5	//
6	// Asserts, on a REAL mounted FUSE filesystem:
7	//   1. A shared-scope-exit delete on a node with an active covering grant
8	//      completes successfully (no EIO).
9	//   2. Exactly ONE rotate_read_from_node rotation is published for the
10	//      grant-root (assert via an IPNS sequence-number bump of exactly +1 on
11	//      the grant-root's own ipnsName).
12	//   3. A revoked recipient can no longer read the rotated subtree (the old,
13	//      pre-rotation read key AEAD-fails against the post-rotation body).
14	//   4. A private delete (no covering grant anywhere in the ancestor chain)
15	//      produces ZERO rotation publishes -- the parent's read key is
16	//      UNCHANGED after the delete (only a plain relink republish occurs).
17	//
18	// MOUNT-INIT DEPENDENCY (read before diagnosing an EIO): the scope-exit gate
19	// (`gate_scope_exit`/`run_scope_exit_gate`, `crates/fuse/src/write_ops/
20	// grant_scope.rs`) fails CLOSED (EIO) until `CipherBoxFS.sent_shares` is
21	// AUTHORITATIVE, i.e. until a `/shares/sent` refresh has succeeded this
22	// session. The desktop mount now seeds that cache at mount init (bounded,
23	// best-effort) and drives a 30s periodic background refresh
24	// (`spawn_periodic_sent_shares_refresh`, wired in `fuse/mod.rs` +
25	// `fuse/windows/mod.rs`). So a delete/rename issued in the first moments
26	// after mount -- before the init refresh completes, or if it failed and the
27	// periodic task has not yet recovered -- can transiently EIO by design; the
28	// retry loops below (6 attempts x 15s) absorb that window. A PERSISTENT EIO
29	// across all retries means either the init+periodic refresh is failing to
30	// reach the relay (check the desktop log for "sent-shares refresh failed")
31	// or a genuine gate regression -- NOT the old "never-wired" gap, which is
32	// now closed. This script fails loudly and diagnostically, never silently.
